ChatSched — Claude fixes, Sept 8
=================================

Scope: this file documents ONLY the eighth task resolved from
"Claude To fix 1..txt" — item 12, HIGH: Opportunity acceptance is not
atomic. Items 5 through 11 were already completed and documented
separately ("claude fixes Sept 1" through "Sept 7"). Item 13 was left
untouched.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. What the finding said, verified against the real source
------------------------------------------------------------------------
src/pages/BusinessOpportunities.tsx's decide() function, on accept:
  1. UPDATEs opportunity_applications.status = 'accepted' as its own,
     independent request.
  2. THEN, as a completely separate request, inserts the actual
     requests/channel_requests row — with the insert's result never even
     checked (`await supabase.from("requests").insert(...)`, no
     destructured {error}, no error handling of any kind).
If step 2 fails for any reason, step 1 has already committed. The
finding's own framing ("application can become accepted while the actual
booking/request creation fails") is exactly right.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. What I found while tracing this, beyond the finding's own description
------------------------------------------------------------------------
Read the trigger that step 1's UPDATE actually depends on before writing
anything, since the finding doesn't mention it: close_out_accepted_
opportunity() (schema_phase69_opportunity_multi_accept.sql), an AFTER
UPDATE trigger on opportunity_applications.status, fires the instant step
1 commits — and once enough slots are filled, it ALSO declines every
other pending application on that opportunity and marks the opportunity
itself 'filled'. This means the real blast radius of step 2 failing is
larger than "one application shows accepted with no booking" — if it was
the last needed slot, every OTHER applicant has already been declined
and the opportunity has already closed, all before the failed booking
insert is even attempted.

I also found a second, related race the finding's own wording doesn't
name directly, but its required fix's own step 1 ("locks the
application") implies needs covering: for an opportunity needing more
than one publisher (publishers_needed > 1), two concurrent accept calls
for two DIFFERENT pending applications on the SAME opportunity could
each check "is there still a slot open" before either commits, and both
proceed — overbooking the opportunity beyond what it actually needed.
Locking only the application row (as the finding's own step 1 literally
says) doesn't prevent this, since the two calls touch two different
application rows; the opportunity row itself has to be locked too, since
that's the actual shared, contended resource.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. The fix
------------------------------------------------------------------------
supabase/schema_phase89_atomic_opportunity_acceptance.sql (NEW):
  - accept_opportunity_application(application_id) — a single RPC
    implementing the finding's own five numbered steps exactly:
    1. Locks the application (SELECT ... FOR UPDATE).
    2. Verifies state — locks the opportunity too (closing the multi-slot
       race above), checks the caller is the opportunity's own business
       or an admin, checks the application is still pending, checks the
       opportunity is still open, and re-counts accepted applications
       against publishers_needed rather than trusting any cached count.
    3. Creates the request/channel_request — identical field mapping to
       what BusinessOpportunities.tsx used to do client-side, just
       executed here instead.
    4. Updates the application to 'accepted' — the same write that fires
       close_out_accepted_opportunity(), completely unchanged; it now
       just runs inside the same transaction as the booking insert
       above, rather than as an already-committed, separate request.
    5. Commits as one unit via the function's own implicit transaction —
       any exception anywhere above rolls back everything, including the
       status update and its trigger cascade. There is no longer a state
       where an application shows accepted (or other applicants show
       declined, or the opportunity shows filled) with no actual booking
       behind it.
  - PUBLIC execute revoked, granted only to authenticated — same
    defense-in-depth convention established in item 7 and continued in
    item 11.

src/pages/BusinessOpportunities.tsx (EDITED):
  - decide()'s accept branch now calls this RPC once and checks its
    result, surfacing any rejection (not pending, not open, already
    fully booked, not your opportunity) as an actual error message
    instead of silently doing nothing. The decline branch is unchanged —
    it was already a single, safe UPDATE with no dependent insert, and
    the finding's own scope names only acceptance as the atomicity
    problem.

supabase/tests/atomic_opportunity_acceptance_test.sql (NEW):
  - pgTAP test file, 9 assertions. A true race between concurrent accept
    calls can't be simulated inside one pgTAP transaction (same
    limitation as item 11's test file) — what it proves is that the
    sequential state transitions are correct: an unrelated business can't
    accept on an opportunity it doesn't own; the real owner accepting a
    social-media publisher's application creates a real requests row and
    marks it accepted in one call; accepting that same application again
    fails; accepting a second (non-social-media) application creates a
    real channel_requests row, AND close_out_accepted_opportunity's
    existing cascade correctly fires from inside that same
    transaction — the opportunity flips to filled and the still-pending
    third application is auto-declined, proving the cascade and the
    booking insert now genuinely share one atomic unit.

supabase/tests/README.md (EDITED): added a section documenting how to
run the new test file and what it covers.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. Why this shape, not a different one
------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Named and shaped exactly as the finding's own required fix specifies
  (accept_opportunity_application(), the same five numbered steps) —
  there was no ambiguity to resolve here the way there was for items 8
  and 10, so the fix follows the finding's own design directly rather
  than substituting a different architecture.
- Locking the opportunity row in addition to the application, beyond
  what the finding's own step 1 literally names, because the multi-slot
  overbooking race is real (confirmed by reading
  close_out_accepted_opportunity() and publishers_needed's own
  schema_phase69 history) and the finding's required fix already implies
  it should be covered — "locks the application" was the closest
  existing instruction to build from, not a ceiling on what needed
  locking.
- Left the decline path completely untouched, because it was never the
  problem: a single UPDATE with no dependent row creation doesn't have
  an atomicity gap to close, and touching it would be scope creep beyond
  what "opportunity acceptance is not atomic" actually names.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. What's NOT done, and why that's a deliberate boundary here
------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Did not add a business-subscription-active check to the new RPC, even
  though the ORIGINAL opportunity could only have been posted with one
  (opportunities_insert_own's own gate) — under the once-off membership
  model (item 10), subscriptions essentially never lapse anymore except
  via admin revoke, and adding a check here would be scope creep beyond
  this finding's atomicity-only concern, consistent with the same
  decision made for item 11's credit RPCs.
- Item 13 in "Claude To fix 1..txt" is untouched.
- Claude to fix 2.txt and Claude to fix 3.txt were not opened.
- Same standing honesty note this project's test suite has carried since
  it began: no Postgres, Docker, or Supabase CLI in this sandbox, so
  neither the new migration nor the new test file has been run against a
  real database. Everything here was reviewed by hand, line-by-line,
  against the actual current trigger and frontend code — not written
  from memory of what it "should" say. The first real `supabase test db`
  run, and a genuine concurrent-request test (a load-testing tool firing
  two overlapping accept calls for different applications on the same
  multi-slot opportunity, not two sequential manual ones), are the
  actual tests of whether this is correct.
